Follow recommendations -
def __init__(self):
self.events={} 

Methods Required 
1.	Register participant
Function Prototype: 
def register_participant(self, event_id: str, count: int) -> dict 
Example Input:
register_participant({}, "Gaming", 10)
Expected Output:
{'Gaming': 10}
Implementation Flow:
•	Check if the event already exists; if so, increase the count
•	Otherwise, add the event to the dictionary.
•	Return the updated dictionary 
  
2. cancel_participant(event_id: str, count: int) -> dict 

Function Prototype:
def cancel_participant(self, event_id: str, count: int) -> dict 
Example Input:
cancel_participant({'Gaming': 20}, "Gaming", 5)
Expected Output:
{'Gaming': 15}
Implementation Flow:
•	Check if the event doesn't exists;or the number of participants registered for the event are less than the number of participants to cancel; return an error - raise ValueError (Cannot cancel more participants than registered)
•	Update the events registration by subtraction of count
•	Return the updated dictionary.
  
3. reschedule_event(old_event_id: str, new_event_id: str) -> dict 

Function Prototype:
def reschedule_event(self, old_event_id: str, new_event_id: str) -> dict 
Example Input:
reschedule_event({'Gaming': 20}, "Trekking", 5)
Expected Output:
{'Trekking': 25}
Implementation Flow:
•	If old event id is not in the events dictionary return the existing dictionary
•	Set count = old event id value in events dictionary
•	If new event id is in events dictionary, update new event value by addition of count
•	else set new event id value as count.
•	delete old event id
•	Return the updated dictionary
  
4. get_active_events() -> list 
•	Return event_ids where participant count > 0 
•	Return list of strings 

Function Prototype:
def get_active_events(self) -> list 
Example Input:
get_active_events({'Gaming': 15, 'Trekking': 25})
Expected Output:
['Gaming', 'Trekking']
Implementation Flow:
•	Iterate over the dictionary and include only events with count > 0.
•	Return a list of available events

OOPS Easy: Smart City Traffic Control System
Problem Statement
The Smart City Traffic Control System monitors and manages traffic flow at multiple intersections. Each intersection has:
•	Key → Intersection Name
•	Value → Vehicle Count
The system supports:
•	Adding intersections
•	Updating vehicle counts
•	Identifying congested areas
•	Dynamically adjusting traffic signals
Class Creation
Create the class with following instructions
class TrafficControlSystem
follow the following recommendation
def __init__(self:):
self.traffic_data = {}
Operations
1. Add a New Intersection
Registers a new intersection with its initial vehicle count.
Function Prototype:
def add_intersection(self, intersection: str, vehicle_count: int) -> dict:
Example Input:
add_intersection({}, "5th Avenue & Main St", 20)
Expected Output:
{'5th Avenue & Main St': 20}
Implementation Flow:
•	Insert intresection as key and vehicle count as value
•	Return the updated dictionary.
2. Update Vehicle Count
Modifies the vehicle count at a specified intersection.
Function Prototype:
def update_vehicle_count(self, intersection: str, new_count: int) -> dict:
Example Input:
update_vehicle_count({'5th Avenue & Main St': 20}, "5th Avenue & Main St", 35)
Expected Output:
{'5th Avenue & Main St': 35}
Implementation Flow:
•	If intersection is not found in traffic data dictionary return error as "Error: Intersection not found"
•	Otherwise update intersection with new count input
•	Return updated traffic dictionary
3. Identify Congested Intersections
Finds intersections where vehicle count exceeds a given threshold.
Function Prototype:
def get_congested_intersections(self, congestion_threshold: int) -> dict:
Example Input:
get_congested_intersections({'5th Avenue & Main St': 35, 'Broadway & 1st St': 10}, 30)
Expected Output:
{'5th Avenue & Main St': 35}
Implementation Flow:
•	For intersection with vehicle count > congestion threshold, return intersection and vehicle count entry as dictionary
4. Adjust Traffic Signals Based on Congestion
Dynamically adjusts signal timing based on congestion levels.
Function Prototype:
def adjust_traffic_signals(self) -> dict:
Example Input:
adjust_traffic_signals({'5th Avenue & Main St': 85, 'Broadway & 1st St': 50, 'Central Square': 25})
Expected Output:
{'5th Avenue & Main St': 'Long Green', 'Broadway & 1st St': 'Normal Green', 'Central Square': 'Short Green'}
Implementation Flow:
•	Declare empty dictionary
•	Use intersection as keys and for values :
•	For count>80 "Long Green"
•	For 40-80 "Normal Green"
•	Otherwise "Short Green"
